WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.550
In this video, I will be explaining what Discrete
Mathematics is, and why it's important for

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.550
the field of Computer Science and Programming.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.730
Discrete Mathematics is a branch of mathematics
that deals with discrete or finite sets of

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.580
elements rather than continuous or infinite
sets of elements.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.250
Imagine trying to run a program that requires
an infinite number of executions to complete

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.250
a task.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.300
It's obvious to say, that the program would
run forever and the task would never be completed

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.020
because there is an infinite number of executions.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:33.699
In order to avoid this problem, we approximate
the continuous sets with discrete sets.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:39.170
Now you may be thinking, I never use math
that involves infinite sets, but I promise

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:40.659
that you do.

00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:43.280
The simplest example is with a circle.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:50.929
A circle by definition is an infinite number
of points equally distant from a fixed point.

00:00:50.929 --> 00:00:55.839
The problem with this is that if we try to
write a program that prints out all of these

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:59.979
points, it will run forever because there
is an infinite number of points and therefore

00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:02.489
an infinite number of executions.

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:09.390
So, this is physically impossible, that's
why if we zoom in here, you can see that when

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.260
you come down here, there is all these points,
but in reality we should have even more points

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.720
between these points.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.050
And if we zoom in on those, we should have
more points between those points, and we can

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.290
never complete the task.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:27.140
Now we've all seen circles on computers, how
is this possible, because we just established

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.100
that it's impossible.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.860
The answer is, is that there is approximations.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.870
For example, consider regular polygons.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.820
Regular Polygons, like a triangle, or a square,
or a pentagon.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.790
They don't really look like circles.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:47.570
However, if you keep increasing the number
of vertices.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:54.170
Eventually you will get hexagons, octagons,
decagons, hexadecagons, icosagons.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:59.420
You can see that these regular polygons, the
more and more you increase the number of vertices,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.060
which the vertices are equally distant from
a fixed point, they will eventually approximate

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:10.420
a circle, and eventually they will be indistinguishable
to the naked eye and will look identical to

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:10.819
a circle.

